{
  "contract_version": 2,
  "issue": "tracebloc/backend#2220",
  "rfc": "RFC-BACKEND-664 \u00a7P0",
  "source_of_truth": "client-runtime/node_sizing.py::envelope_from_allocatable",
  "regenerate_with": "python3 scripts/gen_envelope_vectors.py",
  "description": [
    "The one definition of 'how much of this machine may a single training run",
    "have'. Before this file the question was answered independently by the bash",
    "installer, its PowerShell twin, the Go CLI, and a fourth 0.75-fraction",
    "policy in node_sizing.py itself \u2014 four implementations, none derived from",
    "the others, two of which disagreed by construction.",
    "",
    "The installer speaks bash, its twin speaks PowerShell and the CLI speaks",
    "Go, so no consumer can call the Python. Each therefore vendors this file",
    "and replays the golden vectors below through its own reader, in its own",
    "test suite, drift-gated against this repo at a pinned ref. Mutate the",
    "arithmetic without regenerating the vectors and this repo reddens at once;",
    "regenerate them and every consumer's gate reddens. That is the ticket's",
    "definition of done."
  ],
  "overhead": {
    "cpu_millicores": 1000,
    "memory_bytes": 3221225472,
    "why": [
      "Reserved for the platform \u2014 kubelet, the k3s server, jobs-manager, the",
      "requests proxy, MySQL \u2014 so a run sized to the ceiling still leaves the",
      "cluster able to schedule and report it. 1 CPU / 3 GiB is what every live",
      "producer already subtracts; keeping it identical is what makes this",
      "ticket a no-op on installed edges rather than a fleet envelope change."
    ]
  },
  "floor": {
    "cpu_millicores": 1000,
    "memory_bytes": 2147483648,
    "why": [
      "Below this a training run is not worth scheduling, so a machine that",
      "cannot clear it after overhead is reported NON-VIABLE. It is not a clamp:",
      "clamping up is how the 4 GiB WSL2 machine ends up asked to host an 8 GiB",
      "pod that stays Pending forever."
    ]
  },
  "vm_reserve": {
    "memory_bytes": 1073741824,
    "why": [
      "Memory inside the Docker VM that is NOT a k3d node container and must",
      "survive whatever the nodes are capped to: the k3d serverlb and tools",
      "containers, dockerd/containerd itself, and enough page cache for image",
      "layers. Measured on a live 2-node cluster: serverlb 39.27 MiB + tools",
      "14.69 MiB, plus the daemon and guest kernel outside any container. 1 GiB",
      "is deliberately more than the containers alone -- capping the nodes to",
      "the last byte of the VM starves the runtime that runs them, which is the",
      "same class of failure as not capping them at all.",
      "",
      "This is a reserve against the VM, NOT the per-pod platform overhead in",
      "`overhead` above. They stack: overhead is subtracted inside a node,",
      "vm_reserve outside all of them."
    ]
  },
  "topology": {
    "question": "How many k3d node containers may this VM honestly host, and what is each one's memory cap?",
    "source_of_truth": "client-runtime/node_sizing.py::honest_topology",
    "issue": "tracebloc/backend#2221",
    "rule": [
      "usable = vm_memory_bytes - vm_reserve.memory_bytes",
      "nodes  = clamp(usable // per_node_minimum.memory_bytes, 1, requested_nodes)",
      "cap    = usable // nodes        (floored -- a cap that rounds up is not a cap)"
    ],
    "vm_source": "docker info {{.NCPU}} and {{.MemTotal}} -- the container runtime's VM, NOT the host and NOT the sum of node allocatable",
    "per_node_minimum": {
      "derived_from": "overhead + floor",
      "cpu_millicores": 2000,
      "memory_bytes": 5368709120,
      "why": [
        "A node container hosts the platform overhead AND must still clear the",
        "training floor, or nothing schedules on it. Derived, never a fourth",
        "constant. Measured: capping a node at 3g -- under the 3 GiB overhead",
        "alone -- made both nodes of a live cluster report |unschedulable, and",
        "the `exact-floor` vector (2 cpu / 5 GiB -> viable) sits exactly here."
      ]
    },
    "cpu_cappable": false,
    "cpu_why": [
      "MEASURED on k3d v5.9.0 / k3s v1.35.5 / Docker 29.5.2: capacity.cpu cannot",
      "be capped by ANY container-level mechanism.",
      "",
      "  * k3d 5.9.0 has no CPU flag at all -- only --servers-memory/--agents-memory.",
      "  * docker --cpus=2 sets a CFS quota (cpu.max 200000 100000 in the",
      "    container) and capacity.cpu stays 10.",
      "  * docker --cpuset-cpus=0-4 makes nproc report 5 and capacity.cpu stays 10.",
      "",
      "cadvisor counts /sys/devices/system/cpu/present and /proc/cpuinfo, neither",
      "of which any cgroup namespaces. Confirmed against a forced node",
      "de-register/re-register, so it is not a stale node object.",
      "",
      "Consequence: memory is made honest by CAPPING, cpu only by having FEWER",
      "NODE CONTAINERS. That is why honest_topology reports cpu_honest rather",
      "than implying the cap closed it, and why collapsing to one node -- the",
      "remedy the GPU path already chose for --gpus=all -- is the general fix."
    ],
    "measured": {
      "host": "10 cpu / 17179869184 B (16.00 GiB)",
      "docker_vm": "10 cpu / 8321712128 B (7.75 GiB)",
      "each_node_uncapped": "10 cpu / 8126672Ki -- byte-identical to the VM MemTotal",
      "kubernetes_believes": "20 cpu / 15.50 GiB == 2.000x",
      "capped_3g_each": "capacity.memory 3221225Ki per node; sum 6.14 GiB <= 7.75 GiB VM (0.792x)",
      "also_double_counted": "ephemeral-storage: 474095688Ki capacity on both nodes, the same VM disk twice"
    }
  },
  "fallback_literal": {
    "cpu": "2",
    "memory": "8Gi",
    "why": [
      "The historical fixed envelope, still hardcoded in six physical places",
      "across three repos (_TRAINING_DEFAULT, the ps1's two inline copies, the",
      "chart template's two, DEFAULT_JOB_RESOURCES). Recorded here so those",
      "become readers too. NOTE it is LARGER than the floor: handing it to a",
      "non-viable machine is the sub-8GiB bug, not a safe default."
    ]
  },
  "anchors": {
    "largest": {
      "question": "What is the largest run this machine could host?",
      "rule": "the single schedulable node maximising (cpu_millicores, memory_bytes) lexicographically",
      "used_by": [
        "client installer \u2014 sizing the value it writes",
        "cli resources set \u2014 clamping the wizard prompt and the fit check"
      ],
      "why": [
        "A pod's resources all come from ONE node, so this question is",
        "single-node by nature and must never sum across the cluster.",
        "The tie-break is itself a consolidation: bash ranked nodes (memory,",
        "cpu) and cli's nodeLarger ranked them (cpu, memory), so on a cluster of",
        "8c/16Gi + 4c/32Gi the two anchored on different nodes. Nobody chose",
        "that; it fell out of two independent implementations."
      ]
    },
    "every": {
      "question": "What envelope fits on EVERY schedulable node?",
      "rule": "minimum cpu_millicores and minimum memory_bytes, taken independently",
      "used_by": [
        "client-runtime derive path (DERIVE_JOB_ENVELOPE, off by default)"
      ],
      "why": [
        "An envelope sized to the biggest node cannot schedule on the smallest.",
        "Independent minima are deliberately conservative \u2014 a heterogeneous",
        "cluster with a small system nodepool degrades toward the floor rather",
        "than risking an unschedulable pod."
      ]
    }
  },
  "rendering": {
    "Gi": "cpu=<whole cores>,memory=<whole GiB>Gi \u2014 installer + CLI surface",
    "Mi": "cpu=<whole cores>,memory=<whole MiB>Mi \u2014 jobs-manager pod spec",
    "rounding": "always floor; a ceiling that rounds up is not a ceiling"
  },
